"""Original concise teaching authored against the supplied 2026 module evidence.
Each seed encodes a worked scenario, distractors and an active recall target.
This is a curated starter bank, not exhaustive LOS coverage.
"""
import json,pathlib
C=json.load(open('src/data/curriculum.json'));records=[]
def add(mid,name,explanation,example,trap,prompt,choices,answer,why,formula=None):
 m=next(m for t in C for m in t['modules'] if m['id']==mid);t=next(t for t in C if t['id']==m['topicId']);cid=mid+'-c'+str(1+sum(c['moduleId']==mid for c in records))
 source={'file':t['file'],'pdfPage':m['pdfPage'],'endPdfPage':m['endPdfPage'],'section':m['title']}
 q={'id':cid+'-q1','conceptId':cid,'topicId':t['id'],'moduleId':mid,'prompt':prompt,'choices':choices,'answer':answer,'explanation':why,'distractors':[why if i!=answer else 'This applies the principle correctly.' for i in range(3)],'difficulty':2,'type':'scenario' if t['id']=='v10' else ('calculation' if any(x.isdigit() for x in prompt) else 'application'),'source':source}
 records.append({'id':cid,'moduleId':mid,'topicId':t['id'],'name':name,'objective':'Explain and apply '+name.lower()+'.','explanation':explanation,'why':why,'definition':explanation.split('. ')[0]+'.','example':example,'trap':trap,'mnemonic':name+' → '+trap,'recall':'Without looking: explain '+name.lower()+' and one mistake to avoid.','recallAnswer':explanation+' Watch for: '+trap,'source':source,'questions':[q],**({'formula':formula} if formula else {})})
def f(name,expression,variables,intuition,example,mistake,related=[]):return {'name':name,'expression':expression,'variables':variables,'intuition':intuition,'when':intuition,'example':example,'mistake':mistake,'related':related}
# Quantitative Methods
add('v1-m1','Compounding versus averaging','Arithmetic return describes the average single-period outcome. Geometric return describes the constant compounded rate that links initial and final wealth. Money-weighted return also responds to the timing and size of external cash flows.','An investment rises 20% and then falls 20%: 100 becomes 120 then 96. Arithmetic mean is 0%; geometric mean is √0.96 − 1 = −2.02%.','Equal percentage gains and losses do not cancel in wealth.','A fund returns +20% and −20% over two years, with no flows. Its annual compound return is closest to:', ['0.00%','−2.02%','−4.00%'],1,'Multiply 1.20 by 0.80, then take the square root and subtract one. −4% is the cumulative, not annualized, return.',f('Geometric mean return','R_g = [Π(1 + R_t)]^(1/n) − 1','R_t: each period return; n: number of equal-length periods','Use to link wealth through multiple periods.','1.20 × 0.80 = 0.96; √0.96 − 1 = −2.02%.','Do not average the percentage returns arithmetically.'))
add('v1-m2','Discounting future cash','Present value converts a future payment into its equivalent value today at the required return. Cash flows must be placed on a timeline and discounted for their own number of periods.','A payment of 1,210 in two years at 10% is worth 1,210 / 1.10² = 1,000 today.','Match the rate period to the cash-flow period.','You will receive 1,210 in two years. At an annual required return of 10%, present value is:', ['1,000','1,100','1,464.10'],0,'Discount twice: 1,210 / 1.21 = 1,000. 1,100 discounts only one period; 1,464.10 compounds instead.',f('Present value','PV = FV / (1 + r)^n','FV: future cash; r: rate per period; n: number of periods','Discount an isolated future cash flow.','1,210 / 1.10² = 1,000.','A higher discount rate lowers present value.'))
add('v1-m3','Correlation and causation','Correlation measures linear co-movement on a scale from −1 to +1. Zero correlation rules out linear association, not every possible relationship. Outliers can distort the measure.','If Y = X² and X is symmetrically distributed around zero, the relationship is deterministic but linear correlation can be zero.','Correlation alone does not establish causation.','An analyst finds zero correlation between two variables. Which conclusion is justified?', ['No relationship of any kind exists','There is no measured linear association','One variable cannot help explain the other'],1,'Zero correlation is about linear association. Nonlinear relationships may remain, and causal conclusions require other evidence.')
add('v1-m4','Updating probabilities with evidence','Bayes’ rule combines a prior probability with the likelihood of new evidence. The evidence must be normalized across all possible underlying states.','Default prior 10%; warning occurs in 80% of defaults and 20% of non-defaults. P(default | warning) = 0.08 / (0.08 + 0.18) = 30.77%.','Do not reverse conditional probabilities.','A warning appears in 80% of defaults and 20% of non-defaults. Prior default probability is 10%. After a warning, default probability is closest to:', ['80.00%','30.77%','8.00%'],1,'Joint default-and-warning probability is 0.10 × 0.80 = 0.08. Total warning probability is 0.08 + 0.90 × 0.20 = 0.26.',f('Bayes update','P(A|B) = P(B|A)P(A) / P(B)','A: event of interest; B: evidence; |: conditional on','Update an initial belief using observed evidence.','0.08 / 0.26 = 30.77%.','The likelihood P(B|A) is not the posterior P(A|B).'))
add('v1-m5','Portfolio risk and covariance','Expected portfolio return is the weighted average of expected asset returns. Risk also depends on covariance: imperfectly correlated assets can reduce volatility when combined.','Two equally weighted assets each have 20% volatility and zero correlation. Portfolio variance = 0.25×0.04 + 0.25×0.04 = 0.02; volatility = 14.14%.','Average volatility is generally not portfolio volatility.','Two assets have 20% volatility, zero correlation and equal weights. Portfolio volatility is closest to:', ['20%','10%','14.14%'],2,'Take the square root of weighted variances plus twice weighted covariance. Zero correlation removes covariance, not the individual variances.',f('Two-asset portfolio variance','σ_p² = w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂σ₁σ₂ρ₁₂','w: weights; σ: volatility; ρ: correlation','Combine individual risk and co-movement.','Equal weights, 20% volatility and zero correlation give σ_p = √0.02.','Square weights and take the final square root.'))
add('v1-m6','Simulation versus bootstrap','Monte Carlo draws simulated inputs from specified distributions. Bootstrap samples repeatedly with replacement from observed data. Neither method repairs a poor model or unrepresentative history.','An analyst repeatedly samples 60 monthly observations from the same historical dataset, allowing repeats. This is bootstrap resampling.','Bootstrap draws from the observed sample, not necessarily a chosen normal distribution.','An analyst resamples historical returns with replacement to estimate uncertainty. This is:', ['Bootstrap resampling','A census','Simple random sampling without replacement'],0,'Replacement allows the same observation to appear more than once in a resample. A census uses every population member.')
add('v1-m7','Precision of a sample mean','The standard error measures variation of the sample mean across repeated samples. Under independent sampling with finite variance, it decreases with the square root of sample size.','If population standard deviation is 12 and n = 36, standard error is 2. With n = 144, it is 1.','Four times the sample size halves standard error; doubling does not.','To halve the standard error of a mean, all else equal, sample size should be:', ['Doubled','Quadrupled','Halved'],1,'Standard error is σ/√n. Multiplying n by four doubles the denominator.',f('Standard error of the mean','SE = σ / √n','σ: population standard deviation (or estimate s); n: independent observations','Quantify precision of the sample mean.','12 / √36 = 2.','Do not confuse standard error with dispersion of individual observations.'))
add('v1-m8','Type I error and test power','A Type I error rejects a true null hypothesis. A Type II error fails to reject a false null. Power is the probability of rejecting a false null, equal to one minus the Type II error probability.','A test with Type II error probability 25% has 75% power.','Failure to reject is not proof that the null is true.','A test has a 25% probability of failing to reject a false null. Its power is:', ['25%','75%','95%'],1,'Power is 1 − β = 75%. A significance level such as 5% concerns Type I error, not this probability.')
add('v1-m9','Independence in contingency tables','A chi-square test compares observed category counts with expected counts under independence. Expected counts use row total times column total divided by the grand total.','A row has 40 observations, a column has 30, and the table has 100. The expected count at their intersection is 12.','Use counts, not the category labels as numeric measurements.','In a table of 100 observations, row total is 40 and column total is 30. Expected count under independence is:', ['70','12','0.12'],1,'40 × 30 / 100 = 12 expected observations. 0.12 is the joint proportion, not the count.')
add('v1-m10','Interpreting a regression slope','In simple linear regression, the slope estimates the change in the dependent variable associated with a one-unit increase in the explanatory variable. The intercept predicts Y at X = 0. Association alone is not causation.','If predicted Y = 2 + 1.5X, at X = 4 the predicted outcome is 8.','Keep units consistent; a slope is not automatically an elasticity.','A fitted model is Ŷ = 2 + 1.5X. At X = 4, predicted Y is:', ['6','8','14'],1,'Substitute X = 4: 2 + 1.5 × 4 = 8. Omitting the intercept produces 6.',f('Simple linear prediction','Ŷ = b₀ + b₁X','Ŷ: prediction; b₀: intercept; b₁: slope; X: explanatory input','Predict using an estimated linear model.','2 + 1.5 × 4 = 8.','Prediction outside the estimation range is risky.'))
add('v1-m11','Learning from labeled data','Supervised learning fits a relationship using labeled outcomes. Unsupervised learning searches for structure without supplied outcome labels. Training success must be checked on unseen data to detect overfitting.','A model trained on loans labeled default/no default is supervised; clustering firms without labels is unsupervised.','High training accuracy is not evidence of reliable out-of-sample performance.','A model is trained using borrowers with known default outcomes. The learning method is:', ['Unsupervised clustering','Supervised learning','A guarantee of causal inference'],1,'The observed default labels make this supervised learning. Labels do not establish causality or eliminate overfitting.')
# Economics
add('v2-m1','Shutdown versus breakeven','A price-taking firm can continue producing in the short run when price covers average variable cost, even if it does not cover total average cost. Fixed costs still exist if it shuts down.','Price 9, AVC 7 and ATC 11: each unit contributes 2 toward fixed costs, so production can minimize the short-run loss.','A loss does not automatically imply immediate shutdown.','A competitive firm has price 9, AVC 7 and ATC 11 at its optimal output. In the short run it should:', ['Shut down because profit is negative','Continue because price exceeds AVC','Expand without regard to marginal cost'],1,'Production covers variable costs and part of fixed costs. Output still follows marginal conditions; continuation does not justify unlimited output.')
add('v2-m2','Business-cycle indicators','Leading indicators tend to turn before the economy, coincident indicators move with it, and lagging indicators respond later. A single indicator is not a complete cycle diagnosis.','A rise in new orders before production accelerates is a leading signal.','Indicator timing differs from the direction of its relationship.','Manufacturers’ new orders rise before actual production. In this description, new orders are:', ['Lagging','Coincident','Leading'],2,'Their change precedes production, making them leading in this scenario. Leading does not mean perfectly predictive.')
add('v2-m3','Fiscal stimulus and implementation lags','Fiscal policy uses government spending and taxation to influence the economy. Discretionary changes face recognition, decision and implementation delays; automatic stabilizers respond without new legislation.','During recession, tax collections fall and unemployment benefits rise under existing rules, supporting demand automatically.','A wider deficit can reflect the cycle rather than a new discretionary stimulus.','Unemployment benefits rise in recession under unchanged legislation. This is best described as:', ['An automatic stabilizer','A new monetary-policy decision','Necessarily discretionary fiscal expansion'],0,'The existing fiscal rules generate the response without a new policy vote. Central banks do not determine these benefit payments.')
add('v2-m4','Monetary transmission','Policy rates affect borrowing, spending, asset prices and exchange rates through several channels. Effects take time and depend on credit conditions and expectations.','A policy-rate increase can raise loan costs and reduce interest-sensitive investment.','Rate changes do not mechanically produce an immediate, equal change in inflation.','Which is a conventional channel for tighter monetary policy?', ['Lower borrowing costs stimulate all spending','Higher borrowing costs restrain investment','Inflation instantly falls by the rate increase'],1,'Higher financing costs can reduce investment and aggregate demand. The response is delayed and uncertain, not one-for-one.')
add('v2-m5','Geopolitical transmission into investments','Geopolitical risks affect investment through trade, financial flows, supply chains and confidence. Scenario analysis examines possible exposures rather than assigning certainty to political predictions.','Export restrictions on a key input can raise an importer’s costs even when its sales are entirely domestic.','Domestic sales do not eliminate international supply-chain risk.','A domestic manufacturer imports a critical mineral from a sanctioned country. Its most direct exposure is:', ['Only foreign-currency translation of sales','Supply disruption and input-cost pressure','No geopolitical exposure'],1,'Production depends on the restricted input. Domestic customer location does not remove this operational exposure.')
add('v2-m6','Tariffs and quotas','Tariffs tax imports; quotas restrict quantities. Both can raise domestic prices and protect producers while hurting consumers. A tariff produces government revenue; quota rents depend on who receives import rights.','A tariff of 5 on 1,000 imported units collects 5,000, assuming those imports still occur.','A quota does not automatically deliver the equivalent revenue to government.','Compared with a quota given free to importers, a tariff directly generates:', ['Government tax revenue','No consumer costs','Unlimited imports at the original price'],0,'Tariff payments go to government. Quota rents can instead accrue to license holders, while both restrictions can raise prices.')
add('v2-m7','Exchange-rate quotation','An exchange rate is a price: units of one currency per unit of another. A rising domestic-currency price of foreign currency means domestic depreciation. Reciprocal percentage changes differ.','If INR per USD rises from 80 to 84, USD appreciates 5% in INR terms; INR loses 4.76% measured in USD.','State the quotation before describing appreciation.','The quote moves from INR 80/USD to INR 84/USD. The USD change measured in INR is:', ['5% appreciation','5% depreciation','4.76% appreciation'],0,'84/80 − 1 = 5%. The reciprocal INR depreciation is 80/84 − 1 = −4.76%.')
add('v2-m8','Covered interest parity','A currency forward reflects spot and the interest rates of both currencies under covered no-arbitrage assumptions. The higher-interest-rate currency trades at a forward discount in reciprocal value terms.','For INR/USD spot 80, one-year INR rate 6% and USD rate 4%, forward is 80 × 1.06/1.04 = 81.5385 INR/USD.','Invert the interest-rate ratio if you invert the currency quote.','Spot is 80 domestic per foreign unit. Domestic rate 6%, foreign rate 4%. One-year forward is closest to:', ['78.49','81.54','80.00'],1,'For domestic units per foreign unit, multiply spot by (1+r_domestic)/(1+r_foreign).',f('Covered forward FX rate','F_(d/f) = S_(d/f) × (1 + r_d T) / (1 + r_f T)','d/f: domestic units per foreign unit; T: years under simple-rate convention','Price covered currency delivery using matched interest conventions.','80 × 1.06 / 1.04 = 81.5385.','Do not swap the numerator and denominator.'))
# Corporate issuers
add('v3-m1','Limited liability and ownership','A corporation is legally distinct from its owners. Shareholder losses are generally limited to the investment, while ownership transfer and access to capital differ between private and public corporations.','A fully paid shareholder can lose the entire share investment without ordinarily owing the company’s unpaid trade debts personally.','Limited liability does not protect the value of the investment.','A shareholder has fully paid for ordinary shares in a corporation that fails. Limited liability generally means:', ['The original investment is guaranteed','Personal liability is limited to the investment','Creditors have no claims on corporate assets'],1,'The share investment may be lost. Creditors retain claims on corporate assets; limited liability separates ordinary shareholder personal exposure.')
add('v3-m2','Debt holders versus equity holders','Lenders have contractual claims, while shareholders hold residual claims. Shareholders may favor risky projects with upside even when the additional downside harms lenders.','An already highly leveraged firm substitutes a volatile project for a stable one. Equity may benefit from upside while debt absorbs greater default risk.','A project favored by shareholders need not improve creditor value.','Increasing asset risk after borrowing most directly creates a conflict between:', ['Lenders and shareholders','Two customers buying the same product','The firm and all parties equally'],0,'Fixed debt claims capture limited upside but bear larger default losses. Residual equity can gain from increased volatility.')
add('v3-m3','Principal–agent conflicts','An agency conflict arises when someone with delegated decision authority pursues personal interests that diverge from the principal’s interests. Monitoring, board oversight and aligned incentives can reduce the conflict.','A chief executive buys a larger aircraft for personal convenience despite negligible business benefit.','An incentive based on one metric can create a new distortion.','Which action most directly addresses executive self-dealing?', ['Eliminating independent oversight','Independent board review of related-party decisions','Rewarding revenue regardless of profitability'],1,'Independent scrutiny can limit actions benefiting managers at shareholders’ expense. Revenue-only rewards can encourage value-destroying growth.')
add('v3-m4','Cash conversion cycle','The cash conversion cycle estimates the time between paying suppliers and collecting cash from customers. It combines inventory days and receivable days, then subtracts payable days.','Inventory days 50 + receivable days 30 − payable days 40 = 40 days of cash tied up.','Payables reduce the cycle; they are not added.','Inventory days are 50, receivable days 30 and payable days 40. Cash conversion cycle is:', ['120 days','60 days','40 days'],2,'50 + 30 − 40 = 40 days. Supplier credit finances part of the operating cycle.',f('Cash conversion cycle','CCC = DIO + DSO − DPO','DIO: inventory days; DSO: receivable days; DPO: payable days','Measure how long operations tie up cash.','50 + 30 − 40 = 40 days.','A shorter cycle is not always worth damaging supplier or customer relationships.'))
add('v3-m5','Net present value and relevant cash flows','NPV discounts incremental after-tax cash flows at an appropriate required return and subtracts the investment. Sunk costs are excluded; opportunity costs and effects on existing business matter.','Pay 100 now to receive 121 in two years at 10%: NPV = 121/1.1² − 100 = 0.','Past research spending is sunk, not a new incremental project outflow.','A project needs 100 now and returns 121 in two years. Required return is 10%. NPV is:', ['21','0','10'],1,'Discount 121 for two periods to 100, then subtract 100. Undiscounted profit is not NPV.',f('Net present value','NPV = Σ[CF_t / (1 + r)^t]','CF_t: incremental after-tax cash flow including time zero; r: required return','Evaluate value added by an investment.','−100 + 121/1.10² = 0.','Exclude sunk costs and avoid double-counting financing costs.'))
add('v3-m6','Weighted-average cost of capital','WACC combines required debt and equity returns using appropriate capital weights, usually market values. Deductible interest reduces the after-tax debt cost when the tax shield is usable.','Debt weight 40%, debt cost 5%, tax 25%, equity weight 60%, equity cost 10%: WACC = 7.5%.','Apply the tax adjustment to debt, not equity.','Using 40% debt at 5%, 60% equity at 10% and tax 25%, WACC is:', ['8.0%','6.0%','7.5%'],2,'0.4 × 5% × 0.75 + 0.6 × 10% = 7.5%. 8% ignores the tax shield.',f('WACC','WACC = w_d r_d(1 − T) + w_e r_e','w: market-value weights; r: required costs; T: applicable tax rate','Discount appropriately matched operating cash flows.','0.4×0.05×0.75 + 0.6×0.10 = 0.075.','Do not use one corporate WACC for projects with materially different risk.'))
add('v3-m7','Business-model economics','A business model describes customers, the value offered, how it is delivered, and how revenue and costs produce profit. Similar products can operate with very different revenue models.','A software subscription earns recurring fees; a perpetual license relies more heavily on upfront sales.','Revenue growth alone does not establish a viable unit margin.','A subscription firm grows customers but loses more on each additional customer. Which question matters most?', ['Whether the logo is distinctive','Whether lifetime contribution can cover acquisition and service costs','Whether subscription revenue is automatically profitable'],1,'The model must connect customer economics to costs and cash generation. Recurring revenue is not the same as profitable revenue.')
# Financial statement analysis: source year conventions, not live accounting advice.
add('v4-m1','Analysis starts with a purpose','Financial statement analysis begins by defining the decision and required output, then collecting and processing information, interpreting it, communicating conclusions and following up. Notes and audit reports qualify the headline numbers.','Before calculating debt ratios, a lender defines the credit horizon and repayment question.','Do not let readily available ratios decide the analytical objective.','Before choosing ratios for a credit report, an analyst should first:', ['Define the purpose and context of the analysis','Average every available ratio','Assume a clean audit guarantees investment quality'],0,'A clear purpose determines the relevant evidence and methods. An audit concerns financial reporting, not investment attractiveness.')
add('v4-m2','Basic versus diluted EPS','Basic EPS divides earnings available to ordinary shareholders by weighted-average ordinary shares. Diluted EPS considers potentially dilutive securities; antidilutive instruments are excluded.','Net income 120, preferred dividends 20 and weighted shares 50 give basic EPS of 2.','Use weighted-average shares, not simply year-end shares.','Net income is 120, preferred dividends 20 and weighted-average common shares 50. Basic EPS is:', ['2.40','2.00','2.80'],1,'Subtract preferred dividends before dividing: (120 − 20)/50 = 2.',f('Basic earnings per share','EPS = (NI − preferred dividends) / weighted-average common shares','NI: net income; denominator: time-weighted ordinary shares','Measure earnings attributable to each ordinary share.','(120 − 20)/50 = 2.','Exclude antidilutive instruments from diluted EPS.'))
add('v4-m3','Goodwill versus identifiable assets','Acquisition goodwill arises when the consideration and other relevant acquisition components exceed the fair value of identifiable net assets acquired. It is not the same as an internally developed reputation.','In a simple 100% acquisition, pay 150 for identifiable net assets with fair value 120: goodwill is 30.','A strong internally created brand is not automatically recognized as goodwill.','In a simple cash acquisition of all shares, price is 150 and fair value of identifiable net assets is 120. Goodwill is:', ['270','120','30'],2,'Goodwill is the 30 residual in this simplified transaction. It is separate from identifiable net assets.')
add('v4-m4','Indirect operating cash flow','The indirect method reconciles net income to operating cash flow by adjusting for noncash expenses, nonoperating items and operating working-capital changes. An increase in receivables consumes cash relative to accrual revenue.','Net income 50 + depreciation 10 − receivables increase 8 = CFO 52, with no other adjustments.','Increasing operating liabilities generally adds cash; increasing operating assets generally uses cash.','Net income 50, depreciation 10 and accounts receivable increase 8 are the only adjustments. CFO is:', ['68','52','32'],1,'Add back noncash depreciation and subtract the uncollected receivable increase: 50 + 10 − 8 = 52.')
add('v4-m5','Free cash flow to the firm','FCFF is cash available to both debt and equity providers after operating needs and capital investment. Starting from CFO, add after-tax interest when interest was deducted in CFO, then subtract capital expenditure.','CFO 90, interest paid and included in CFO 10, tax rate 30%, capex 40: FCFF = 90 + 7 − 40 = 57.','The CFO-based formula depends on where interest is classified.','CFO is 90 after interest of 10. Tax is 30%, capex 40. FCFF is:', ['50','60','57'],2,'Restore the after-tax interest cash flow to all providers: 90 + 10(1 − .30) − 40 = 57.',f('FCFF from CFO','FCFF = CFO + Interest(1 − T) − Capex','CFO: operating cash after interest; T: tax rate; Capex: capital expenditure','Estimate cash available to debt and equity when interest is included in CFO.','90 + 10×0.70 − 40 = 57.','If CFO excludes interest, do not add it back again.'))
add('v4-m6','Inventory methods in rising prices','With rising input costs and stable or growing inventory quantities, FIFO generally reports lower cost of goods sold and higher ending inventory than LIFO. The supplied curriculum distinguishes IFRS, which prohibits LIFO, from US GAAP.','Buy one unit at 10 and one at 14; sell one. FIFO COGS is 10 and remaining inventory is 14.','State price and inventory-quantity assumptions; LIFO liquidation changes the comparison.','A firm buys units at 10 then 14 and sells one. FIFO ending inventory per remaining unit is:', ['10','12','14'],2,'FIFO assigns the earliest cost to the sale, leaving the newest unit at 14. An average method would produce 12.')
add('v4-m7','Capitalizing versus expensing','Capitalizing an eligible cost creates an asset and allocates expense over future periods. Immediate expensing lowers current profit more, with no future depreciation of that cost. Total cash paid is not changed by the accounting choice.','A 100 asset with a five-year life and no residual value produces 20 annual straight-line depreciation, rather than 100 immediate expense.','Capitalization does not create cash.','Compared with immediate expensing, capitalizing a cost generally causes initial-period:', ['Higher assets and higher profit','Lower assets and higher profit','Higher total cash receipts'],0,'Some expense is deferred through the asset, raising current profit and assets. The payment itself is unchanged.')
add('v4-m8','Defined contribution versus defined benefit','A defined contribution plan specifies employer contributions. A defined benefit plan promises a benefit, exposing the employer to actuarial and investment assumptions in measuring its obligation and funded status.','Plan assets 90 and defined benefit obligation 110 give a funded deficit of 20.','A contribution payment is not the same as the total pension obligation.','A defined benefit plan has assets 90 and obligation 110. Funded status is:', ['20 surplus','20 deficit','200 obligation'],1,'Funded status = assets − obligation = −20. The deficit differs from both annual contributions and the gross obligation.')
add('v4-m9','Temporary differences and deferred tax','Temporary differences between accounting carrying amounts and tax bases can cause future taxable or deductible amounts. A deferred tax liability represents tax expected to become payable when a taxable difference reverses.','Equipment carrying amount 100, tax base 70 and tax rate 30% give a taxable temporary difference of 30 and DTL of 9.','Permanent differences do not create deferred taxes.','An asset has carrying amount 100 and tax base 70. At 30% tax, the resulting deferred tax liability is:', ['30','9','21'],1,'The taxable temporary difference is 30; multiply by 30% to get 9. Do not multiply the entire carrying amount.')
add('v4-m10','Reporting quality versus earnings quality','Reporting quality concerns faithful, useful financial information. Earnings quality concerns sustainability and adequacy of performance. Truthfully reported weak earnings can have high reporting quality but poor earnings quality.','A company transparently reports a large one-off gain while recurring operations deteriorate. Disclosure can be sound even when reported profit is not sustainable.','An unqualified audit does not establish sustainable earnings.','Transparent reporting of a large nonrecurring gain most directly requires an analyst to question:', ['Automatically the honesty of the accounts','The sustainability of earnings','Whether cash exists in any form'],1,'Separate faithful reporting from recurring economic performance. A disclosed gain is not automatically fraud.')
add('v4-m11','DuPont decomposition','The three-part DuPont identity separates return on equity into net profit margin, total asset turnover and financial leverage. A higher ROE can reflect more leverage rather than better operations.','Margin 10%, turnover 1.2 and equity multiplier 2 produce ROE 24%.','A higher ROE is not always a lower-risk improvement.','Net margin 10%, asset turnover 1.2 and equity multiplier 2 imply ROE of:', ['12%','24%','20%'],1,'Multiply all three components: .10 × 1.2 × 2 = .24.',f('Three-part DuPont ROE','ROE = (NI / Sales) × (Sales / Avg assets) × (Avg assets / Avg equity)','NI: net income; use consistent average balance-sheet quantities','Diagnose whether profitability, efficiency or leverage drives ROE.','10% × 1.2 × 2 = 24%.','Do not interpret leverage-driven ROE as pure operating improvement.'))
add('v4-m12','Integrated forecasting','A financial model links operating assumptions to income, working capital, investment and financing. Sales growth can require additional cash investment even when profits increase.','At a constant receivables/sales ratio of 20%, sales rising from 500 to 600 increase receivables from 100 to 120, tying up 20 cash.','Growth in accounting profit is not identical to growth in free cash flow.','Sales rise from 500 to 600 while receivables stay at 20% of sales. The receivables change uses cash of:', ['120','20','100'],1,'Receivables rise from 100 to 120. Only the increase of 20 is the new working-capital use.')
# Equity
add('v5-m1','Market versus limit orders','A market order prioritizes execution at available prices. A limit order specifies a maximum purchase price or minimum sale price, controlling price while risking nonexecution.','A buy limit of 50 cannot execute at 51, but it can execute at 49 if liquidity is available.','A limit is not a guarantee of execution.','An investor will buy only at 50 or below. Which order best expresses this?', ['A buy limit at 50','An unrestricted market buy','A guaranteed execution at 50'],0,'The buy limit caps the acceptable price. Market orders do not impose that cap, and execution is not guaranteed.')
add('v5-m2','Index weighting','Price-weighted indexes emphasize higher share prices. Market-capitalization-weighted indexes emphasize larger market values. Equal weighting periodically restores equal proportions.','A share at 100 has twice the price-weighted influence of a share at 50 before divisor adjustments, regardless of shares outstanding.','A high share price does not necessarily mean a large company.','Which stock has the greatest influence in a simple price-weighted index?', ['The highest share price','Always the largest market capitalization','Always the highest trading volume'],0,'Price weighting uses prices in the numerator. Market capitalization and volume are different weighting criteria.')
add('v5-m3','Forms of market efficiency','Weak-form efficiency incorporates past market data; semi-strong form incorporates public information; strong form also incorporates private information. Stronger forms include weaker forms.','If public earnings news is rapidly reflected in prices, trading on the already public announcement does not reliably earn abnormal risk-adjusted profit.','Efficient does not mean every observed price is permanently correct.','Semi-strong efficiency most directly challenges persistent abnormal returns from:', ['Already public financial statement information','Information unavailable to anyone','Every possible private-information advantage'],0,'Public statements are included in semi-strong efficiency. Private information belongs to the stronger form.')
add('v5-m4','Residual equity claims','Ordinary shareholders generally have residual claims after creditors and preferred claims. Preferred shares typically have specified dividend and liquidation preferences but are not identical to debt.','If liquidation assets barely cover creditors, common shareholders may receive nothing even when historical book equity was positive.','Book equity is not a guaranteed liquidation payout.','In liquidation, common shareholders generally receive:', ['Payment before secured lenders','The residual after higher-priority claims','A guaranteed book-value payment'],1,'Common equity is residual. Prior claims can exhaust realizable assets.')
add('v5-m5','Revenue drivers and pricing power','Revenue can be decomposed into volume, realized price and mix. Pricing power means sustaining favorable prices without an offsetting loss of demand or competitive position.','Volume rises 10% and average price rises 5%: revenue increases 1.10 × 1.05 − 1 = 15.5%.','Price and volume changes compound rather than simply add.','Volume rises 10% and average price 5%, with no other changes. Revenue growth is:', ['15.0%','15.5%','5.0%'],1,'Multiply the factors: 1.10 × 1.05 = 1.155. The interaction contributes another 0.5 percentage point.')
add('v5-m6','Industry forces and profitability','Industry structure affects how value is split among firms, customers, suppliers and potential entrants. Strong buyer power can pressure margins even when an industry grows quickly.','A supplier dependent on one giant retailer may have little ability to raise prices.','Industry growth and industry profitability are different questions.','A manufacturer sells nearly all output to one large buyer with many alternative suppliers. The most direct pressure is:', ['Strong buyer bargaining power','Guaranteed supplier pricing power','No competitive pressure'],0,'The buyer can switch sources and negotiate aggressively, reducing the manufacturer’s bargaining position.')
add('v5-m7','Forecasts as explicit assumptions','Forecasts should connect operating drivers to financial outcomes and test alternative scenarios. A scenario changes a coherent set of assumptions rather than pretending the base case is certain.','A recession case combines lower volume with weaker pricing and slower customer collections.','Do not vary revenue alone if costs and working capital also respond.','Which forecast best handles an uncertain downturn?', ['One precise forecast with no assumptions','Coherent scenarios linking demand, margins and cash needs','Assuming historical growth must repeat'],1,'Scenarios expose sensitivity to economic mechanisms. Precision without justified assumptions is not reliable prediction.')
add('v5-m8','Constant-growth dividend valuation','The Gordon model discounts a perpetual dividend stream growing at a constant rate. Required return must exceed growth, and the numerator is next period’s dividend.','Next dividend 3, required return 10%, growth 4%: value = 3/.06 = 50.','Do not use the just-paid dividend without growing it once.','Next year’s dividend is 3, required return 10% and perpetual growth 4%. Value is:', ['30','50','75'],1,'Use D1/(r−g) = 3/(.10−.04) = 50. Ignoring growth gives 30.',f('Gordon growth value','V₀ = D₁ / (r − g), with r > g','D₁: next dividend; r: equity required return; g: perpetual growth','Value a stable perpetual dividend stream.','3 / (.10 − .04) = 50.','The model is invalid with g ≥ r.'))
# Fixed income
add('v6-m1','Covenants in an indenture','The indenture sets contractual bond terms. Affirmative covenants require actions; negative covenants limit actions that can harm lenders.','Providing audited statements is affirmative; restricting additional secured borrowing is negative.','The words affirmative and negative describe obligations, not whether the covenant is helpful.','A covenant limits new secured borrowing. It is:', ['An affirmative covenant','A negative covenant','An equity voting right'],1,'It restricts an issuer action. An affirmative covenant would require a specified action.')
add('v6-m2','Embedded options and cash flows','Callable bonds give the issuer the right to redeem early under specified terms. Putable bonds give the investor a right to demand repayment. These rights change the risk and required yield.','When rates fall, an issuer may call a high-coupon bond and refinance, reducing the investor’s upside.','A call option benefits the issuer; a put option benefits the investor.','A fall in yields makes a bond’s issuer more likely to exercise which provision?', ['Investor put','Issuer call','A ban on refinancing'],1,'A call permits refinancing expensive debt. It exposes investors to reinvestment and upside-limitation risk.')
add('v6-m3','Primary versus secondary bond markets','Primary issuance raises capital for the issuer. Secondary trades transfer outstanding securities between investors. Dealer-based trading and issue-specific liquidity are important in many bond markets.','An investor buys an existing bond from another investor: the issuer receives no new financing from that trade.','Trading volume is not the same as new capital raised.','A pension fund buys an outstanding corporate bond from an insurer. This is:', ['Primary issuance','A secondary-market transaction','New equity capital'],1,'Ownership of existing debt changes; the original issuer does not raise new funds in this exchange.')
add('v6-m4','Repo financing','A repurchase agreement economically functions as collateralized borrowing through a sale and promised repurchase. A haircut makes collateral value exceed cash advanced. Collateral mitigates but does not eliminate risk.','Collateral worth 100 with a 5% haircut supports 95 cash borrowing.','Collateral is not a guarantee against counterparty loss.','Securities worth 100 secure a repo with a 5% haircut on collateral value. Cash advanced is:', ['105','100','95'],2,'Cash advanced is 100 × (1−.05) = 95. The excess collateral provides a buffer.')
add('v6-m5','Government debt is not uniform','Government borrowers differ in taxing powers, monetary flexibility, currency of debt and institutional strength. Sovereign status alone does not make all debt risk-free.','A government owing foreign-currency debt cannot simply create that foreign currency to meet payments.','Separate local-currency capacity from foreign-currency obligations.','Which feature most directly constrains a sovereign’s payment flexibility?', ['Debt denominated in a currency it cannot issue','Having a domestic tax base','Publishing debt statistics'],0,'Foreign-currency liabilities require foreign-currency resources. Monetary sovereignty does not cover every currency.')
add('v6-m6','Bond price and yield','For fixed promised cash flows, price and yield move inversely. A bond trades below par when its coupon rate is below its required yield, assuming consistent periodic conventions.','A one-year bond pays 5 coupon plus 100 principal. At 6% yield its price is 105/1.06 = 99.06.','Coupon rate is not yield to maturity.','A one-year annual-pay bond has face 100, coupon 5 and yield 6%. Price is closest to:', ['100.94','99.06','105.00'],1,'Discount the single payment of 105 at 6%. A below-market coupon implies a discount.',f('Bond present value','P = Σ[C / (1 + y)^t] + FV / (1 + y)^N','C: coupon per period; y: yield per period; N: periods; FV: redemption value','Price fixed cash flows on a coupon date.','One period: (5+100)/1.06 = 99.06.','Use yield and coupons for the same payment frequency.'))
add('v6-m7','Yield spreads and embedded options','A yield spread measures extra yield relative to a benchmark but can reflect several risks. Option-adjusted spread removes the modeled value of embedded options from the spread measure.','A callable bond’s option-adjusted spread can be lower than its zero-volatility spread because some spread compensates investors for the issuer call.','Not all spread is pure expected default loss.','For a callable bond with a valuable issuer call, which relationship is most typical?', ['OAS below Z-spread','OAS always equal to coupon rate','OAS above Z-spread solely because of the call'],0,'Removing the cost of the issuer’s call reduces the remaining spread. This separates option compensation from other spread components.')
add('v6-m8','Floating-rate quoted and required margins','A floating-rate coupon resets to a reference rate plus a quoted margin. If the market-required margin rises above the quoted margin, the instrument tends to trade below par, all else equal.','A floater promises reference + 100 basis points, but investors now require reference + 160 basis points. Its contracted compensation is insufficient at par.','A reset reference rate does not eliminate issuer credit-spread risk.','Quoted margin is 100 bps and required margin rises to 160 bps. The floater tends to trade:', ['Above par','At par regardless of credit','Below par'],2,'Its coupon margin is less than the market requires. Price must fall to improve the investor’s expected yield.')
add('v6-m9','Spot rates and forward rates','A spot rate discounts a single cash flow at its maturity. Forward rates link investment periods under no-arbitrage compounding. A forward rate is not a guaranteed forecast of the future spot rate.','One-year spot 4% and two-year spot 5% imply year-two forward = 1.05²/1.04 − 1 = 6.0096%.','Use the full compounded spot accumulation factors.','Annual spot rates are 4% for one year and 5% for two years. The one-year forward starting in one year is closest to:', ['1.00%','6.01%','5.00%'],1,'(1.05)² = 1.04(1+f). Solving gives 6.0096%.',f('Implied one-year forward','1 + f₁,₂ = (1 + s₂)² / (1 + s₁)','s₁: one-year spot; s₂: two-year spot; f₁,₂: year-two forward','Link annual compounded spot rates without arbitrage.','1.05²/1.04 − 1 = 6.0096%.','A forward rate is a pricing implication, not a certain forecast.'))
add('v6-m10','Investment horizon and duration','Bond investors face price risk and coupon reinvestment risk. For a small parallel yield change and appropriate assumptions, matching the horizon to Macaulay duration approximately balances these risks.','A long-horizon investor can benefit from reinvesting coupons at higher rates even though the bond price initially falls.','A price decline alone does not describe the entire holding-period return.','An investor’s horizon matches Macaulay duration. A small parallel yield shift approximately:', ['Eliminates credit risk','Balances price and reinvestment effects','Guarantees no market-price change'],1,'Duration matching addresses the offset between price and reinvestment risk under its assumptions. It does not remove credit risk or price fluctuations.')
add('v6-m11','Modified duration','Modified duration approximates proportional price sensitivity to a small yield change, with cash flows held fixed. The negative sign reflects the inverse price–yield relationship.','Duration 6 and a 0.50% yield increase imply about −6 × .005 = −3% price change before convexity.','Enter 50 basis points as 0.005, not 0.50.','Modified duration is 6. Yield rises 50 bps. Approximate price change ignoring convexity is:', ['−3%','+3%','−300%'],0,'ΔP/P ≈ −D×Δy = −6×.005 = −.03.',f('Duration price estimate','ΔP/P ≈ −D_mod × Δy','D_mod: modified duration; Δy: decimal yield change','Approximate fixed-cash-flow bond sensitivity to small yield moves.','−6 × .005 = −3%.','Duration is a local linear approximation.'))
add('v6-m12','Convexity correction','Convexity captures curvature missed by duration. For positive convexity, the second-order adjustment adds to the duration estimate for either an upward or downward yield move.','Duration 5, convexity 40 and yield increase .01 give −.05 + .5×40×.0001 = −4.8%.','Square the yield change and include the one-half factor.','Duration 5, convexity 40 and yield rise 1% imply approximate return:', ['−5.2%','−4.8%','−3.0%'],1,'−5×.01 + .5×40×(.01)² = −.048.',f('Duration plus convexity','ΔP/P ≈ −D Δy + ½ Conv (Δy)²','D: duration; Conv: convexity; Δy: decimal yield change','Improve a duration-only price estimate.','−.05 + .002 = −4.8%.','Positive convexity adds value in both directions, under this approximation.'))
add('v6-m13','Effective versus key rate duration','Effective duration reprices a bond when the benchmark curve shifts, allowing option-dependent cash flows to change. Key rate duration isolates sensitivity at particular curve maturities.','A callable bond’s expected redemption date may change when yields fall, so fixed-cash-flow modified duration misses part of the response.','A parallel-shift measure does not fully describe a curve twist.','To examine exposure to a rise at only the five-year point of the curve, use:', ['Key rate duration','Only maturity in years','Coupon rate alone'],0,'Key rate duration isolates a curve segment. Aggregate duration primarily summarizes a specified broad shift.')
add('v6-m14','Expected credit loss','Expected loss combines probability of default, exposure at default and loss given default. Loss given default is one minus the recovery fraction on that exposure.','Exposure 1,000, default probability 2%, recovery 40%: expected loss = 1,000 × .02 × .60 = 12.','Recovery rate is not loss given default.','Exposure 1,000, default probability 2% and recovery 40% imply expected loss:', ['8','20','12'],2,'Loss fraction is 60%, so expected loss is 12. Using recovery produces 8, the wrong quantity.',f('Expected credit loss','EL = PD × LGD × EAD','PD: default probability; LGD: loss fraction conditional on default; EAD: exposure','Estimate probability-weighted credit loss over a specified horizon.','0.02 × 0.60 × 1,000 = 12.','Use matching horizons and distinguish expected from unexpected loss.'))
add('v6-m15','Sovereign ability and willingness to pay','Sovereign credit analysis considers resources and flexibility as well as institutions and willingness to honor obligations. Debt ratios alone do not capture political or external financing vulnerability.','Two governments with equal debt/GDP can differ in reserve adequacy and dependence on foreign funding.','Capacity and willingness are distinct credit dimensions.','Two countries have identical debt/GDP. Why might credit risk differ?', ['Debt/GDP determines all sovereign risk','Institutions and external funding exposure differ','Sovereigns never default'],1,'Institutional quality, revenue flexibility, currency structure and external resources all affect repayment risk.')
add('v6-m16','Debt priority and coverage','Corporate credit analysis combines business risk, leverage, coverage and claim priority. Secured debt has claims on pledged assets, but collateral value and legal enforcement still matter.','EBIT 80 and interest expense 20 give EBIT interest coverage of 4 times.','A collateral pledge does not ensure full recovery.','EBIT is 80 and interest expense 20. EBIT interest coverage is:', ['0.25×','4×','60×'],1,'80/20 = 4. Coverage is a ratio of income to interest, not their difference.',f('Interest coverage','Coverage = EBIT / Interest expense','EBIT: earnings before interest and tax','Compare operating earnings with interest burden.','80/20 = 4×.','Coverage is not cash flow and does not capture principal repayments.'))
add('v6-m17','Securitization participants','Securitization pools financial assets and issues claims backed by their cash flows. An originator supplies assets, an SPE holds them in a separate structure, and a servicer administers collections.','A lender sells a loan pool to an SPE, which issues securities whose payments depend on the pool.','The servicer and the legal owner of the assets need not be the same entity.','Who typically administers borrower collections in a securitization?', ['The servicer','Every bondholder individually','Only the rating agency'],0,'Servicing handles collections and administration. The SPE holds the assets; rating agencies assess credit risk.')
add('v6-m18','Credit enhancement and subordination','Subordination allocates losses to junior claims before senior claims. It redistributes pool credit risk across tranches rather than eliminating the underlying borrowers’ losses.','A 100 pool has 10 junior and 90 senior notes. An 8 loss is absorbed by junior claims in a simple sequential loss structure.','Tranching changes who bears risk, not whether losses can occur.','A pool has 10 junior and 90 senior claims. With an 8 loss allocated junior-first, the senior loss is:', ['8','0','7.2'],1,'The junior buffer absorbs the 8 loss. Senior protection holds here because losses do not exceed 10.')
add('v6-m19','Mortgage prepayment risk','Mortgage borrowers may repay ahead of schedule. Falling rates often accelerate refinancing and shorten expected mortgage-security cash flows; rising rates can slow prepayments and extend them.','When rates fall, principal returns faster and must be reinvested at lower yields.','Contraction risk occurs with faster prepayments; extension risk with slower prepayments.','Rates fall sharply and refinancing accelerates. Mortgage investors most directly face:', ['Contraction and reinvestment risk','Longer cash flows from slower prepayment','No change because legal maturity is fixed'],0,'Borrower prepayment shortens realized cash flows despite unchanged legal maturity.')
# Derivatives
add('v7-m1','Exchange-traded versus OTC contracts','Exchange-traded derivatives are standardized and commonly centrally cleared. OTC contracts allow customization. Central clearing changes counterparty exposure and collateral requirements but does not remove market risk.','A firm needing an unusual settlement date may prefer an OTC forward to a standardized futures contract.','Clearing does not insure against adverse market moves.','A firm needs a highly customized currency settlement date. Which instrument most directly accommodates it?', ['A tailored OTC forward','Only a standardized listed future','A risk-free speculative position'],0,'Bilateral customization is a key OTC feature. Customization does not mean the position is risk-free.')
add('v7-m2','Option payoff versus profit','An option holder has a right, not an obligation. A call pays the excess of asset price over strike at expiry when positive. Profit also subtracts the premium paid, ignoring financing in a simple expiry calculation.','Buy a call for 4 with strike 50. At expiry stock is 60: payoff 10, profit 6.','Payoff excludes premium; profit includes it.','A call costs 4, strike is 50 and expiry price is 60. Buyer profit ignoring financing is:', ['10','6','−4'],1,'max(60−50,0) − 4 = 6. Ten is the payoff before premium.',f('Long call expiry profit','Profit = max(S_T − K, 0) − c₀','S_T: expiry asset price; K: strike; c₀: premium (financing ignored)','Separate exercise payoff from total purchase economics.','max(60−50,0)−4 = 6.','A positive payoff can coexist with a loss after premium.'))
add('v7-m3','Hedging versus eliminating all risk','A hedge offsets a specified exposure but can leave basis, liquidity, counterparty or operational risks. The hedge direction should follow the underlying economic exposure.','A wheat producer expecting to sell harvest can short wheat futures to offset falling wheat prices.','A hedge against one risk may create another.','A producer will sell wheat later and fears a price decline. A conventional hedge is:', ['Long wheat futures','Short wheat futures','Buy more unhedged wheat'],1,'A short futures position gains as futures prices fall, offsetting lower revenue on the physical sale, subject to basis risk.')
add('v7-m4','Cash-and-carry pricing','No-arbitrage forward pricing links spot cost, financing, storage and benefits of holding the underlying. The forward price is a delivery price, not simply the investor’s forecast.','For a non-income asset costing 100, no storage cost and a one-year 5% financing rate, forward price is 105.','Separate financing costs from expected price appreciation.','A non-income asset costs 100. Annual financing is 5%, with no other carry. One-year no-arbitrage forward price is:', ['95','100','105'],2,'Buy the asset and finance it for one year: repayment is 105.',f('Forward price with no income','F₀(T) = S₀(1 + r)^T','S₀: spot; r: annual effective financing rate; T: years','Price delivery of a non-income asset without other carry costs.','100 × 1.05 = 105.','Income and storage require appropriate carry adjustments.'))
add('v7-m5','Forward price versus forward value','A forward’s delivery price is fixed at initiation. Its value subsequently changes with spot and financing conditions. An at-market forward normally begins at zero value before fees or special terms.','An old long forward delivery price of 100 becomes valuable if an otherwise identical new forward price rises to 110.','The contract delivery price does not reset just because market value changes.','The current fair forward price rises above the delivery price of an existing long forward. The existing long generally has:', ['Positive value','A higher contractual delivery price automatically','Always zero value until expiry'],0,'The holder has locked in a cheaper delivery price. Positive value and unchanged contract price can coexist.')
add('v7-m6','Daily settlement and financing','Futures gains and losses are settled daily. Forward settlement generally occurs at the contracted date. The correlation between futures price changes and interest rates can affect their relative pricing.','If futures gains arrive when rates are high, those gains can be reinvested favorably compared with waiting to receive them at maturity.','Equal final asset exposure does not imply identical cash-flow timing.','What most directly distinguishes ordinary futures from otherwise similar forwards?', ['Daily marking to market and settlement','No market risk','An option holder’s right to walk away'],0,'Daily settlement creates interim cash flows and liquidity needs. Futures remain binding commitments with market risk.')
add('v7-m7','Swap rate versus swap value','The fixed rate agreed on a swap is its price term; the present value of remaining net cash flows is its current value. A plain at-market swap starts near zero value, but its value changes as curves move.','A fixed-rate receiver benefits, all else equal, when market fixed swap rates fall below the contracted fixed rate.','A zero initial value does not mean zero future risk.','After initiation, market fixed swap rates fall. A receiver of the old higher fixed rate generally:', ['Gains value','Loses value solely because fixed receipts are higher','Must reset its contracted rate'],0,'The received fixed leg is more attractive relative to current market terms. The contract rate remains fixed.')
add('v7-m8','Moneyness and time value','A call is in the money when asset price exceeds strike; a put is in the money when strike exceeds asset price. Option premium can include time value beyond immediate exercise value.','Call premium 8, stock 55 and strike 50: exercise value 5 and time value 3.','In the money does not guarantee a profitable trade after premium.','A call trades at 8 when the stock is 55 and strike 50. Time value is:', ['8','5','3'],2,'Subtract exercise value from premium: 8 − max(55−50,0) = 3.')
add('v7-m9','Put–call parity','European puts and calls with the same strike, expiry and non-dividend-paying underlying satisfy a replication relationship: a call plus discounted strike equals a put plus stock.','If stock 100, present value of strike 95 and call 8, put = 8 + 95 − 100 = 3.','Match strike, expiry, exercise style and underlying cash-flow assumptions.','Stock is 100, PV of strike 95 and European call 8. Under simple no-dividend put–call parity, put value is:', ['13','3','−3'],1,'c + PV(K) = p + S, so p = 8 + 95 − 100 = 3.',f('European put–call parity','c₀ + PV(K) = p₀ + S₀','c₀: call; p₀: put; K: common strike; S₀: non-dividend stock','Replicate equal-expiry European option payoffs.','8 + 95 − 100 = 3 for the put.','Do not apply unchanged to American options or dividend-paying stocks.'))
add('v7-m10','Risk-neutral binomial valuation','A one-period binomial model prices a replicable payoff by discounting its risk-neutral expected payoff at the risk-free rate. The risk-neutral probability is a pricing weight, not a forecast of actual frequency.','Stock 100 moves to 120 or 80, gross risk-free return 1.05. q = (1.05−.8)/(1.2−.8) = .625. A strike-100 call is worth .625×20/1.05 = 11.90.','Use pricing probability and risk-free discounting together.','With up factor 1.2, down .8 and gross risk-free return 1.05, risk-neutral up probability is:', ['50%','62.5%','25%'],1,'(1.05−.8)/(1.2−.8)=.625. Equal-sized moves do not imply a 50% pricing probability.',f('Risk-neutral up probability','q = (1 + r − d) / (u − d)','r: one-period risk-free return; u,d: price multipliers','Price a one-period replicable payoff when d < 1+r < u.','(1.05−.8)/(.4) = .625.','q is not the investor’s real-world forecast.'))
# Alternatives
add('v8-m1','Fund, direct and co-investment','Fund investing delegates selection to a manager and often adds fees. Direct investing gives control but requires expertise and capital. Co-investment provides participation alongside a fund, with its own due-diligence demands.','An investor buys a stake in one private company alongside its fund manager: a co-investment rather than another diversified fund commitment.','Lower fees do not eliminate concentration or execution risk.','An LP invests separately alongside its fund in a single acquisition. This is:', ['A co-investment','A publicly traded index automatically','A guaranteed diversified exposure'],0,'The separate alongside investment is a co-investment and can increase concentration.')
add('v8-m2','Fees and the high-water mark','Alternative-fund returns must reflect the specified management fee, incentive fee and hurdle rules. A high-water mark generally prevents charging incentive fees on the recovery of prior losses.','Prior high-water mark 100; value fell to 90 then recovers to 98. No new high-water-mark profit has been created.','Fee order and the fee base must be read, not assumed.','With a high-water mark of 100, a fund recovers from 90 to 98. Ignoring other terms, incentive fees on this recovery are:', ['Charged on the full 8','Not earned because value remains below 100','Charged on 98'],1,'The prior peak has not been exceeded. Management fees can still apply under the contract.')
add('v8-m3','Private capital and illiquidity','Private equity and private debt commonly commit capital for extended periods with limited exit flexibility. Early fees and investment spending can produce negative early returns before realizations, often described as a J-curve.','An early-stage fund calls capital and incurs costs years before selling portfolio companies.','An early negative return does not alone reveal final fund quality.','The J-curve most commonly refers to:', ['Early negative returns followed by potential later gains','Guaranteed positive returns from day one','Daily liquidity at net asset value'],0,'Fees and early deployment precede mature realizations. Later recovery is possible, not guaranteed.')
add('v8-m4','Real estate income and capitalization','Income-producing property can be valued by capitalizing stabilized net operating income. A higher capitalization rate implies a lower value for the same NOI. Financing cash flows are distinct from property NOI.','Stabilized NOI 120,000 and cap rate 6% imply value 2,000,000.','Do not subtract debt interest when using property-level NOI for direct capitalization.','NOI is 120,000 and cap rate 6%. Direct capitalization value is:', ['720,000','2,000,000','7,200'],1,'Value = NOI/cap rate = 120,000/.06 = 2,000,000.',f('Direct capitalization','Value = Stabilized NOI / Cap rate','NOI: net operating income before financing; cap rate: required capitalization rate','Estimate income-property value under stable-income assumptions.','120,000/.06 = 2,000,000.','Capital expenditure and lease assumptions require careful treatment.'))
add('v8-m5','Commodity futures roll return','A rolling futures strategy replaces expiring contracts. With an upward-sloping curve and otherwise stable conditions, buying the more expensive later contract creates a negative roll effect. Spot, roll and collateral components are distinct.','An investor sells the near contract at 100 and buys the next at 104. If the curve is unchanged as contracts age, convergence can hurt returns.','A positive spot-price change need not imply positive futures total return.','Under a stable upward-sloping futures curve, a long rolling strategy tends to experience:', ['Negative roll return','Guaranteed positive roll return','No effect from replacing contracts'],0,'Buying dearer deferred exposure that converges downward produces negative roll effects under the stated conditions.')
add('v8-m6','Hedge-fund strategy risk','Hedge funds can use short positions, leverage and derivatives, but their risks differ substantially by strategy. Market-neutral targets do not eliminate liquidity, leverage, model or counterparty risk.','A long-short fund can suffer when a crowded short rises sharply even with low estimated market beta.','The label hedge fund does not mean the portfolio is hedged against every risk.','A fund with near-zero equity beta can still face:', ['No meaningful risk','Liquidity and leverage risk','Only risk-free interest income'],1,'Low beta addresses measured market exposure, not all sources of loss.')
add('v8-m7','Digital-asset ownership and custody','Distributed ledgers record transactions across a network. Digital-asset investing adds custody, key management, technology, market and regulatory risks. A token’s rights depend on its design and legal terms.','Losing a private key can make directly held assets inaccessible even when the ledger itself works correctly.','A distributed ledger does not guarantee the economic value of a token.','Which risk remains even when a public ledger records all transfers correctly?', ['Private-key loss and custody failure','Guaranteed redemption at face value','Automatic protection from price volatility'],0,'Ledger integrity does not solve key management or guarantee value or redemption rights.')
# Portfolio management
add('v9-m1','Diversification and the efficient frontier','Combining less-than-perfectly correlated risky assets can reduce risk. The efficient frontier contains portfolios with the highest expected return at each risk level, or lowest risk at each return level.','Two portfolios have equal expected return, but one has lower volatility. A risk-averse mean–variance investor prefers the lower-volatility portfolio.','Diversification benefits depend on co-movement, not merely the number of holdings.','Two portfolios have equal expected return. One has lower volatility. Under mean–variance analysis, the higher-volatility one is:', ['Dominated','Always preferred','Automatically on the efficient frontier'],0,'It offers no extra expected reward for its extra risk, so the lower-risk alternative dominates it.')
add('v9-m2','CAPM and systematic risk','CAPM relates required expected return to beta exposure to the market risk premium. Diversifiable firm-specific risk does not earn an additional premium in the model.','Risk-free 3%, market expected return 8%, beta 1.2: required return = 3% + 1.2×5% = 9%.','Use the market excess return, not the total market return, in the beta term.','Risk-free return 3%, market expected return 8%, beta 1.2. CAPM return is:', ['9.0%','12.6%','6.0%'],0,'3% + 1.2(8%−3%) = 9%. Multiplying beta by 8% fails to remove the risk-free portion.',f('CAPM expected return','E(R_i) = R_f + β_i[E(R_m) − R_f]','R_f: risk-free return; β_i: market sensitivity; E(R_m): expected market return','Estimate a model-based required return for systematic risk.','3% + 1.2×5% = 9%.','CAPM is a model with assumptions, not a promised return.'))
add('v9-m3','Portfolio management as a process','Portfolio management moves from planning through execution to feedback. Monitoring checks whether objectives, constraints, market exposures or holdings have changed enough to require action.','A client’s imminent house purchase changes liquidity needs, so the portfolio plan may need revision even if markets are unchanged.','The portfolio is managed against client needs, not only a market benchmark.','A client unexpectedly needs a large cash payment next year. The manager should first:', ['Reassess objectives and constraints','Ignore it until annual performance reporting','Increase illiquid investments automatically'],0,'The changed liquidity need affects planning and portfolio suitability before execution choices.')
add('v9-m4','Investment policy statement','An IPS documents return and risk objectives alongside constraints such as liquidity, time horizon, taxes, legal limits and unique circumstances. Willingness and ability to take risk can differ.','An investor enjoys speculation but must fund essential spending soon: willingness may be high while capacity is low.','Do not equate enthusiasm for risk with financial ability to bear loss.','An investor has high risk willingness but low capacity because of near-term essential spending. The portfolio should:', ['Be aggressive solely because willingness is high','Respect the lower capacity and clarify objectives','Ignore liquidity constraints'],1,'Capacity constrains feasible risk. Discussion can reconcile preferences, but willingness cannot fund essential obligations.')
add('v9-m5','Cognitive and emotional biases','Cognitive biases involve information processing or beliefs; emotional biases arise from feelings or impulses. Loss aversion can make investors hold losing positions to avoid realizing a loss.','An investor refuses to sell a deteriorating holding because selling would make the loss feel real.','A purchase price is not a valuation argument for holding today.','An investor retains a deteriorating stock solely to avoid realizing a loss. This most closely illustrates:', ['Loss aversion','Perfect Bayesian updating','An absence of behavioral influence'],0,'The decision is driven by the pain of recognizing loss, rather than updated risk and value.')
add('v9-m6','Risk governance and risk budgets','Risk management identifies, measures and modifies exposures within a governance framework. A risk budget allocates permissible risk across activities instead of treating each position independently.','A portfolio can reduce overall concentration by limiting the share of total risk contributed by one strategy, even if capital weights appear balanced.','Equal capital weights need not mean equal risk contributions.','Why can equal capital allocations produce unequal risk contributions?', ['Assets have different volatilities and correlations','Capital weight completely determines risk','Risk is unrelated to holdings'],0,'Risk depends on each exposure’s volatility and interaction with the rest of the portfolio, not only money invested.')
# Ethics: original scenarios, deliberately distinguish lawful from ethical.
add('v10-m1','Ethics beyond minimum legal compliance','Ethical decision making considers stakeholders, duties, conflicts and consequences. Legal permission is not a complete ethical justification, and situational pressures can distort otherwise well-intentioned decisions.','A technically permitted sales practice still hides a material downside from a client. An ethical review asks whether the client can make an informed choice.','Legal does not automatically mean ethical.','A salesperson says a misleading omission is acceptable because no local rule expressly bans it. The best response is:', ['Accept legality as the only test','Consider duties and fair disclosure beyond minimum law','Assume ethical standards never exceed law'],1,'Ethical responsibilities can exceed legal minima. An omission can undermine informed client decisions even if a specific prohibition is absent.')
add('v10-m2','Applying the stricter requirement','When applicable law and professional standards differ, members and candidates must follow the stricter applicable requirement. Knowing participation in violations is not excused by a supervisor’s instruction.','A local rule permits conduct that the Standards prohibit. The member follows the stricter professional requirement.','A manager’s approval does not override a professional obligation.','Local law allows a practice prohibited by the Standards. A candidate should:', ['Follow the stricter applicable requirement','Always follow the less restrictive local rule','Rely solely on supervisor approval'],0,'The stricter applicable rule controls. Permission from a supervisor does not remove individual responsibility.')
add('v10-m3','Material nonpublic information','Information is material when it could affect investment decisions or price; it is nonpublic until available to the market. Members must not act or cause others to act on material nonpublic information.','A director privately discloses an unannounced takeover. An analyst must not trade or tip clients on that information.','A reliable private source can make information more material, not safer to trade.','A director privately reveals a likely unannounced takeover. The analyst should:', ['Buy before publication because the source is reliable','Refrain from trading or tipping on the information','Tell only the firm’s largest client'],1,'Reliability strengthens the information’s significance. Both personal trading and causing clients to act are prohibited here.')
add('v10-m3','Mosaic research','A research conclusion may combine public material information with nonmaterial nonpublic details. The mosaic principle does not permit using a material nonpublic input.','An analyst combines published filings with routine, nonmaterial supplier observations to estimate demand.','Calling a report a mosaic does not sanitize a confidential earnings leak.','Which research process is most consistent with mosaic analysis?', ['Public filings plus nonmaterial private observations','A confidential material earnings number plus public prices','An unpublished takeover plan alone'],0,'The permitted combination excludes material nonpublic information. Public inputs do not neutralize a prohibited confidential input.')
add('v10-m3','Fair dealing with clients','Clients must be treated fairly when recommendations and investment actions are distributed. Fairness does not require identical service packages, but selective timing must not unfairly disadvantage clients.','A new recommendation is sent through a fair distribution procedure rather than first being secretly given to a favored client.','Fair does not necessarily mean identical service, but favoritism in access can violate duties.','An analyst secretly gives an actionable new recommendation to a favored client before normal distribution. The main concern is:', ['Fair dealing','The client’s preferred email font','A requirement that all portfolios be identical'],0,'Preferential early access can disadvantage other clients. Different mandates and disclosed service levels do not justify this favoritism.')
add('v10-m3','Suitability in portfolio context','For an advisory relationship, recommendations should fit the client’s objectives and constraints and be evaluated in the context of the overall portfolio. A suitable asset for one investor may be unsuitable for another.','A high-risk illiquid fund may be inappropriate for a client who needs the invested money for essential spending next month.','Past high returns cannot substitute for suitability analysis.','A client needs the principal next month for essential expenses. Recommending a locked-up speculative fund chiefly raises:', ['Suitability concerns','Only the fund’s naming convention','No concern if last year’s return was high'],0,'The liquidity and risk profile conflict with the client’s needs. Prior returns do not resolve those constraints.')
add('v10-m3','Research independence and gifts','Benefits that could reasonably compromise independence and objectivity must be refused. An issuer’s extravagant benefit tied to favorable coverage threatens unbiased research.','An issuer offers an analyst a luxury vacation if coverage stays positive. The analyst refuses.','Disclosure alone does not make every compromising benefit acceptable.','An issuer offers a lavish trip conditional on favorable research. The analyst should:', ['Accept and merely mention it in small print','Decline the compromising benefit','Accept if the issuer pays rather than the client'],1,'The condition directly compromises objectivity. Disclosure does not cure an explicitly purchased favorable opinion.')
add('v10-m3','Priority of transactions','Client and employer investment transactions take priority over beneficial personal transactions. Personal trading must not exploit advance knowledge of client activity.','An analyst learns a client order will buy a thinly traded security and buys personally first. That prioritizes personal benefit over the client.','A small personal trade can still be front-running.','An analyst buys personally just before placing a known client purchase order. The key issue is:', ['Priority of transactions','Only whether the trade is profitable','No issue if the personal position is small'],0,'Personal front-running uses advance knowledge and can disadvantage the client. Size and eventual profit do not remove the duty.')
add('v10-m3','Diligence and reasonable basis','Recommendations require a reasonable and adequate research basis. Third-party work can be used after assessing its reliability; delegation does not eliminate the analyst’s responsibility.','A manager checks a vendor model’s assumptions, data quality and limitations before relying on its output.','A prestigious provider is not a substitute for due diligence.','An analyst uses a third-party model without checking its assumptions or reliability. The most direct concern is:', ['Diligence and reasonable basis','An absolute ban on outside research','Only portfolio diversification'],0,'External research is not banned, but its reliability must be evaluated sufficiently to support the recommendation.')
add('v10-m4','GIPS compliance and verification','GIPS supports comparable and fair performance presentation. Compliance is claimed by a defined firm, not by an isolated product. Verification assesses firm-wide processes; it does not guarantee a particular portfolio’s return.','A firm cannot represent a single outperforming fund as independently GIPS compliant while ignoring the rest of the defined firm.','Verification is not a guarantee of investment performance.','Which statement is most appropriate?', ['An individual fund alone claims firm-wide GIPS compliance','Verification guarantees future returns','A defined firm claims compliance; verification is not a return guarantee'],2,'Compliance attaches to the defined firm. Independent verification has a process scope and does not insure performance.')
add('v10-m5','Handling a discovered violation','When becoming aware of a violation, a professional must not knowingly participate and may need to dissociate. Escalation and legal advice can help determine appropriate action; an instruction from management does not excuse continued participation.','An employee learns that a performance presentation is knowingly falsified, raises the issue and refuses to distribute it while seeking an appropriate resolution.','Continuing misconduct while waiting indefinitely is not adequate dissociation.','An employee knows a client presentation contains falsified returns. The best action is:', ['Distribute it because management approved it','Refuse participation and take appropriate escalation or dissociation steps','Add an unrelated disclaimer and distribute unchanged'],1,'Knowing distribution continues the violation. Appropriate escalation and dissociation address the misconduct; a generic disclaimer does not cure false returns.')
# Retain module/source traceability and add an independent recall format for every concept.
